# ===========================================================================
#  AppArmor profile for vitrind -- issue #286.
#
#  THIS PROFILE HAS NEVER BEEN LOADED BY ANYONE WHO WROTE IT.
#
#  It was authored on a machine with AppArmor compiled out
#  (/sys/module/apparmor/parameters/enabled = N, Arch, kernel 7.1.8), so it
#  was not parsed, not loaded, not attached and not observed to grant
#  anything. The only instrument that can answer any of that is the
#  `apparmor-profile` job in .github/workflows/ci.yml, which runs on a stock
#  `ubuntu-latest` -- deliberately WITHOUT the
#  `kernel.apparmor_restrict_unprivileged_userns=0` sysctl the `rust` and
#  `integration` jobs take -- installs this file, loads it, spawns a real
#  realm, and then REMOVES the profile and requires the spawn to fail again.
#
#  Until that job has run green, the honest statement about this file is:
#  "a profile is written, and a job exists that will say whether it works."
#  Do not upgrade that sentence anywhere -- docs/book/src/limits.md, README.md,
#  SECURITY.md and site/index.html all carry it in that form on purpose.
#
#  The distinction that keeps the rest of this header honest: this file's
#  BEHAVIOUR is unmeasured, and its FORM is cited. Every structural choice
#  below is copied from a profile that Ubuntu 24.04 actually ships, and the
#  PROVENANCE block names the URL each one came from. Those are different
#  kinds of claim and the header keeps them apart on purpose -- an earlier
#  draft of this file did not, and quoted a form from memory while presenting
#  it as a measurement.
# ===========================================================================
#
# PROVENANCE -- every URL below was fetched on 2026-08-15, and nothing in this
# header is attributed to a source that was not.
#
#   [U1] https://git.launchpad.net/ubuntu/+source/apparmor/plain/profiles/apparmor.d/chrome?h=ubuntu/noble-updates
#   [U2] https://git.launchpad.net/ubuntu/+source/apparmor/plain/profiles/apparmor.d/firefox?h=ubuntu/noble-updates
#   [U3] https://git.launchpad.net/ubuntu/+source/apparmor/plain/profiles/apparmor.d/flatpak?h=ubuntu/noble-updates
#   [U4] https://git.launchpad.net/ubuntu/+source/apparmor/plain/profiles/apparmor.d/unprivileged_userns?h=ubuntu/noble-updates
#   [U5] https://git.launchpad.net/ubuntu/+source/apparmor/plain/parser/apparmor.d.pod?h=ubuntu/noble-updates
#         -- the source of apparmor.d(5) in the same source package.
#   [U6] https://packages.ubuntu.com/noble/amd64/apparmor/filelist
#         -- confirms /etc/apparmor.d/{chrome,firefox,flatpak,unprivileged_userns}
#            are installed by noble's `apparmor` BINARY package, so [U1]-[U4]
#            are shipped files on a 24.04 host and not merely source-tree ones.
#   [U7] https://ubuntu.com/blog/ubuntu-23-10-restricted-unprivileged-user-namespaces
#         -- Ubuntu's own statement of the restriction this file answers.
#   [U8] https://gitlab.com/apparmor/apparmor/-/wikis/unprivileged_userns_restriction.md
#   [U9] https://gitlab.com/apparmor/apparmor/-/wikis/unprivileged_unconfined_restriction.md
#         -- the upstream AppArmor project's own pages on the two restrictions.
#            [U8] is the source for the shipped sysctl defaults and for the
#            behaviour of an `unconfined`-tagged profile; [U9] for what an
#            unprivileged unconfined task's `change_profile` actually does.
#
# [U1], [U2] and [U3] are byte-for-byte the same profile modulo the name and
# the attachment path. [U1] in full, because this file is a copy of its shape:
#
#     # This profile allows everything and only exists to give the
#     # application a name instead of having the label "unconfined"
#
#     abi <abi/4.0>,
#     include <tunables/global>
#
#     profile chrome /opt/google/chrome/chrome flags=(unconfined) {
#       userns,
#
#       # Site-specific additions and overrides. See local/README for details.
#       include if exists <local/chrome>
#     }
#
# What was NOT re-established, and is therefore not cited anywhere below: the
# `bwrap-userns-restrict` profile. An earlier draft of this header attributed a
# no_new_privs/stacking argument to it as "Ubuntu's own". It is not in noble --
# absent from [U6] and 404 on the noble source tree -- so the attribution was
# wrong for the release this file targets, and the argument now rests on [U4],
# which IS shipped there and does the same stacking for the same reason.
#
# WHAT PROBLEM THIS SOLVES
#
# Ubuntu 24.04+ ships `kernel.apparmor_restrict_unprivileged_userns=1`. Under
# it, a task whose AppArmor label is literally `unconfined` may still call
# `unshare(CLONE_NEWUSER)` -- and the kernel then declines to give the new
# namespace the capabilities it is supposed to confer. So the namespace is
# created and the first thing `vitrin-realm-init` does inside it,
# `mount(NULL, "/", NULL, MS_REC|MS_PRIVATE, NULL)`, answers EACCES.
# `vitrind`'s startup preflight measures exactly that pair and refuses to
# start (D-020(6) forbids degrading silently), which is the behaviour this
# project wants and NOT what this file changes.
#
# What this file changes is the prerequisite. Ubuntu did not simply block
# unprivileged user namespaces; it shipped a supported PER-BINARY grant, and
# [U6] shows chrome, firefox and flatpak each already carry one on a 24.04
# host. This is vitrin's.
#
# WHY EACH LINE IS HERE
#
#   abi <abi/4.0>,
#       Pins the policy language this file is written against. [U1]-[U4] all
#       declare this same ABI, and they are the profiles noble ships, so a
#       parser that accepts them accepts this. Upstream's `master` profiles
#       (ABI 5.0) additionally carry `@{exec_path} mr,`; that variable does not
#       exist under abi 4.0 and must NOT be copied back here.
#
#   include <tunables/global>
#       Parity with [U1], [U2] and [U3], which carry this line and nothing else
#       between the ABI and the profile block. It defines the @{...} tunables
#       the parser expects to be in scope.
#
#   profile vitrind /usr/lib/vitrin/{vitrind,vitrin-realm-init} ...
#       The NAME is what makes this work, not the rules. Per [U7]:
#       "unprivileged processes will only be able to create user namespaces if
#       they are confined and have the "userns," rule in their AppArmor profile
#       (or if they have CAP_SYS_ADMIN)". A named profile is what makes the
#       task count as confined for that check.
#
#       The ATTACHMENT is a brace alternation over two binaries on purpose,
#       and it is the single most load-bearing decision in this file. The
#       process that issues the six-flag `unshare` is NOT `vitrind` -- it is
#       `vitrin-realm-init`, which `vitrind` reaches by `execve` (see
#       crates/vitrin-realm-init/src/main.rs, and crates/vitrin-core/src/spawn.rs
#       for the exec). `vitrind` needs the grant too, because its own
#       `--print-isolation` preflight forks a child that calls `unshare`
#       (crates/vitrin-core/src/spawn/isolation.rs). Covering both paths with
#       one profile means the vitrind -> vitrin-realm-init exec attaches to
#       the SAME profile: same label, no transition, and no dependence on what
#       AppArmor does for a child that matches no attachment. That fallback
#       behaviour under `flags=(unconfined)` is not stated in [U5], so this
#       glob is written to make the question moot rather than to bet on the
#       answer.
#
#       `vitrind` resolves its helper as a sibling of its own `current_exe()`
#       (crates/vitrin-core/src/main.rs, `default_realm_init_path`), so
#       installing both binaries into /usr/lib/vitrin/ needs no `--realm-init`
#       flag and keeps the two paths inside one glob.
#
#   flags=(unconfined)
#       Confines NOTHING. [U5] on this mode: "This mode allows a task confined
#       by the profile to behave as though it is unconfined." It exists to give
#       the task a name instead of the label `unconfined`, which is what
#       [U1]-[U3] say in their own words: "This profile allows everything and
#       only exists to give the application a name instead of having the label
#       "unconfined"".
#
#       Two quotes, two sources, and they are kept apart because an earlier
#       draft ran them together and attributed the manual's sentence to the
#       profiles' header comment. Neither says what the other says.
#
#       READ THIS BEFORE COPYING THE FLAG. [U5] also says, of the same mode:
#       "This mode should not be used under regular deployment but can be
#       useful during debugging and some system initialization scenarios", and
#       "true unconfined is being phased out, with unconfined becoming a
#       replaceable profile. As such unconfined mode will be emulated by a
#       special profile compiled with the default_allow flag in newer kernels."
#       So the flag this file uses is one its own manual discourages for
#       deployment -- and Ubuntu ships it in [U1]-[U3] regardless. That tension
#       is real and is not resolved here: the form is chosen to match what the
#       distribution actually ships, because a profile that differs from every
#       shipped example is a fourth thing nobody has tested. `default_allow` is
#       the successor [U5] names, and switching to it is a deliberate future
#       change to be made against a measurement, not a tidy-up.
#
#   userns  (spelled with its trailing comma in the rule below)
#       The grant itself, and the rule [U7] names. [U5] gives the grammar as
#       `USERNS RULE = [ QUALIFIERS ] 'userns' [ USERNS ACCESS PERMISSIONS ]`
#       with `USERNS ACCESS PERMISSIONS = ( 'create' )` -- `create` is the only
#       permission there is, so the bare rule and `allow userns create` cover
#       the same set. [U5]'s own example is the bare form, commented
#       "# Allow all userns perms", and the bare form is what [U1]-[U3] ship.
#
#       THIS RULE IS NOT DECORATION UNDER AN UNCONFINED FLAG, which is the one
#       thing about this file most likely to be "simplified" away by someone
#       reasoning that a profile allowing everything must already allow this.
#       [U8], on profiles tagged unconfined: "Profiles that are tagged as
#       unconfined have their permissions determined entirely by the profile.
#       That is they are not controlled by the sysctl
#       apparmor_restrict_unprivileged_userns nor do they have the exception
#       for privileged tasks." It then gives the two cases explicitly -- "a
#       profile without a user namespace rule will result in a DENIAL despite
#       being tagged unconfined", versus "a profile with a user namespace rule
#       with an unconfined rule can allow creation of user namespace". Deleting
#       this line does not relax the profile; it inverts it.
#
#   include if exists <local/vitrind>
#       The site-override convention [U1]-[U3] each carry, with the same
#       comment. An operator adds rules in /etc/apparmor.d/local/vitrind
#       without editing this file, so a package upgrade does not clobber them.
#
# WHAT IS DELIBERATELY *NOT* HERE
#
#   No attachment for `vitrin-shim`, and none for the app inside a realm.
#   That is not an oversight and it is not a gap -- it is the design:
#   `vitrin-realm-init` writes `/proc/sys/user/max_user_namespaces=0` inside
#   the realm before it execs the shim, so nesting is already denied there by
#   the helper itself. Adding an attachment for either path would also be
#   actively dangerous: `vitrin-realm-init` sets `PR_SET_NO_NEW_PRIVS` before
#   that exec, and under `no_new_privs` AppArmor denies any domain transition
#   it cannot prove is a reduction of privilege. Today that exec is
#   unconfined -> nothing and is always permitted; once `vitrind` is confined
#   it becomes confined -> X, and if a profile attached to the shim's path the
#   `execve` would FAIL. Ubuntu's shipped `unprivileged_userns` profile [U4]
#   deals with the same class of problem by stacking rather than transitioning
#   -- it carries `allow pix /** -> &unprivileged_userns ,`, which keeps
#   children in the same profile. Vitrin's answer is simpler: attach nothing
#   there, so the exec matches no attachment, performs no transition, and
#   inherits.
#
#   No `mount,`, `pivot_root,` or `capability,` rules. Under
#   `flags=(unconfined)` those are expected to be unnecessary. EXPECTED, not
#   measured -- if the CI job reports the realm spawn failing at the mount
#   with the label already naming `vitrind`, this is the first thing to
#   revisit, because that outcome is byte-identical in errno to having no
#   profile at all. [U4] is why: Ubuntu's fallback profile carries
#   `audit deny capability,` alongside `allow userns,`, so it permits the
#   unshare and denies every capability inside it -- which is precisely issue
#   #286's measured signature. A WRONG profile therefore fails exactly like NO
#   profile, and only the label separates them.
#
# THE SECURITY COST, WHICH IS REAL, IS CONDITIONAL, AND IS PUBLISHED
#
# A profile that is `flags=(unconfined)` and grants `userns` is a name any
# local user may try to borrow: `aa-exec -p vitrind -- <anything>` asks to run
# an arbitrary program under this profile, and this profile grants a user
# namespace and restricts nothing else. Installing this file adds one entry to
# the set of names that can be asked for -- alongside chrome, firefox and
# flatpak [U6], which is company, not an excuse.
#
# WHETHER THAT ASK SUCCEEDS DEPENDS ON A SECOND KNOB, and an earlier draft of
# this header got its value backwards. It said
# `kernel.apparmor_restrict_unprivileged_unconfined` is 0 by default, and that
# the borrow therefore just works. On Ubuntu 24.04 with the `apparmor` package
# installed, the opposite is the shipped state: that package ships
# `/usr/lib/sysctl.d/10-apparmor.conf` (its presence is in [U6]), and [U8]
# quotes that file as setting BOTH
# `kernel.apparmor_restrict_unprivileged_userns = 1` AND
# `kernel.apparmor_restrict_unprivileged_unconfined = 1`.
#
# With the second knob at 1, [U9] describes what an unprivileged unconfined
# task's `change_profile` -- which is what `aa-exec -p` performs -- actually
# does: "instead of transitioning to the specified profile it will stack the
# specified profile with unconfined", giving `unconfined -> target_profile//&
# unconfined` rather than `unconfined -> target_profile`, so that "the system
# restrictions are retained by the stacked unconfined profile". The borrow is
# permitted and does not shed the restriction it was trying to shed.
#
# So the honest published statement has a condition in it: the cost is real
# where an operator has set `apparmor_restrict_unprivileged_unconfined` to 0,
# and is mitigated by the stacking behaviour where Ubuntu's shipped value of 1
# is in force. Neither half is measured here -- this file's PROVENANCE is
# citations, not experiments -- which is exactly why the `apparmor-profile` CI
# job records that knob's value as `unconfined_knob` on the runner it uses.
# A published cost that nothing measures is the failure this header exists to
# avoid. docs/book/src/limits.md and SECURITY.md carry the same conditional
# form; if you find one of them stating the cost flatly, it has drifted.
#
# INSTALLING IT
#
# Byte-for-byte what the `apparmor-profile` CI job runs, deliberately: a
# documented recipe that differs from the measured one is a third thing nobody
# has tested. Every other page that mentions installing this file points here
# rather than restating these lines, for the same reason.
#
#   sudo install -d -m 0755 /usr/lib/vitrin
#   sudo install -m 0755 target/debug/vitrind target/debug/vitrin-realm-init \
#       /usr/lib/vitrin/
#   sudo install -m 0644 packaging/apparmor/vitrind /etc/apparmor.d/vitrind
#   sudo apparmor_parser -r /etc/apparmor.d/vitrind      # no reboot needed
#
# COPY THE BINARIES; DO NOT SYMLINK THEM. AppArmor attaches to the resolved
# absolute pathname, and `vitrind` finds its helper through `current_exe()`,
# which is `/proc/self/exe` and is likewise resolved. A symlink at
# /usr/lib/vitrin/vitrind pointing into a build tree therefore attaches this
# profile to NOTHING, silently, and the only symptom is the same EACCES you
# started with.
#
# Verify: `sudo aa-status | grep vitrind` lists the profile, and
# `vitrind --print-isolation` prints an `apparmor.label=` row -- which must
# NAME `vitrind` rather than read `unconfined`, for this file to be doing
# anything at all. It is deliberately not stated as "must read `vitrind`": a
# label is a profile name plus an optional ` (mode)` suffix, and under this
# file's flag the kernel is expected to render `vitrind (unconfined)`. That is
# the string the isolation module's own unit test pins
# (crates/vitrin-core/src/spawn/isolation.rs), so an exact-match instruction
# here would have told operators to look for something the code says they will
# not see.
#
# ONE EDITING RULE FOR THIS FILE'S COMMENTS, and it is load-bearing rather
# than stylistic. `cargo xtask limits-check` anchors the published claim "this
# profile still grants a user namespace" on the LAST LINE OF THE PROFILE
# DECLARATION TOGETHER WITH THE RULE THAT FOLLOWS IT -- attachment, flags,
# opening brace and grant, as one string -- and its comparison collapses ALL
# whitespace in the file into single spaces before matching. The needle is
# spelled out in crates/xtask/src/limits.rs and is deliberately NOT reproduced
# here, which is the whole of the rule: **no comment in this file may restate
# the anchor, in whole or in part, as a contiguous run of text.**
#
# Quoting the rule on its own is fine and this header does it several times.
# What is not fine is a comment in which the profile's opening brace and the
# rule end up adjacent after whitespace collapses -- the quoted upstream
# profile above is safe only because each of its lines begins with a `#`,
# which survives normalisation and sits between them.
#
# This is not a hypothetical, twice over. The needle was ONCE the bare rule,
# and commenting the rule out left the gate green because the prose explaining
# the rule satisfied it. It was then re-anchored on the brace -- and the
# rewritten paragraph describing THAT anchor quoted it verbatim, restoring the
# vacuity in the very sentence warning against it. Both were found the same
# way and it is the only way: comment the rule out, run
# `cargo xtask limits-check`, require RED, restore. Do that after any edit to
# this header, not just after an edit to the rule.

abi <abi/4.0>,

include <tunables/global>

profile vitrind /usr/lib/vitrin/{vitrind,vitrin-realm-init} flags=(unconfined) {
  userns,

  # Site-specific additions and overrides. See local/README for details.
  include if exists <local/vitrind>
}
